#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# limit

#% text
# `limit(f, x -> a)` gives the limit of `f` as `x` tends to `a` along the real numbers.

#% text
# `limit(f, x -> a, direction: from_above)` and `direction: from_below` give the limits from one side.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The point `a` may be a number, a symbolic expression, `inf` or `-inf`.

#% item
# The limit may be `inf` or `-inf`.

#% item
# When the two sides have different limits, the result is `failure(no_limit, limit, f)`.

#% item
# When the limit cannot be decided, the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# Limits the fast rules do not decide go to Gruntz's algorithm (most rapidly varying subexpressions); every finite value is checked numerically near the point before it is returned.

#% item
# `x -> a` may be a list of rules (iterated limits, the last rule first) or `[x, y] -> [a, b]` (decided when the function depends on the variables through one product).

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `direction: from_above` (x decreasing to `a`) or `from_below` (x increasing to `a`).

#% item
# One-sided limits onto the branch cut of `log` and of non-integer powers (the negative reals) take the side of approach into account: `limit(log(-10 + t im), t -> 0, direction: from_below)` is `log(10) - im pi`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A classical limit:

#% cell
limit(sin(x)/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A limit at infinity:

#% cell
limit((1 + 1/x)^x, x -> inf)
#% output
#= e

#% text
# A removable singularity:

#% cell
limit((x^2 - 1)/(x - 1), x -> 1)
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# An infinite limit:

#% cell
limit(1/x^2, x -> 0)
#% output
#= inf

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Indeterminate forms 0 times infinity, 0^0 and infinity minus infinity:

#% cell
[limit(x*log(x), x -> 0), limit(x^x, x -> 0), limit(sqrt(x^2 + x) - x, x -> inf)]
#% output
#= [0, 1, 1/2]

#% text
# Symbolic parameters:

#% cell
[limit((1 + a/x)^x, x -> inf), limit((a^x - 1)/x, x -> 0)]
#% output
#= [exp(a), log(a)]

#% text
# Exponentials and logarithms of different growth, decided by Gruntz's algorithm:

#% cell
[limit(exp(x)*(exp(1/x - exp(-x)) - exp(1/x)), x -> inf), limit((3^x + 5^x)^(1/x), x -> inf)]
#% output
#= [-1, 5]

#% text
# Special functions of a large argument, through their asymptotic expansions:

#% cell
[limit(x*exp(x^2)*erfc(x), x -> inf), limit(factorial(n)*exp(n)/n^(n + 1/2), n -> inf)]
#% output
#= [pi^(-1/2), 2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)]

#% text
# Iterated limits (the last rule first) and a limit in two variables through their product:

#% cell
[limit(real_abs(x)*tan(y)^2, [y -> pi/2, x -> 0]), limit(tan(x*y)/(x*y), [x, y] -> [0, 0])]
#% output
#= [0, 1]

#% text
# Rational functions at infinity:

#% cell
limit((3x^2 + 1)/(2x^2 - x), x -> inf)
#% output
#= 3/2

#% text
# A bounded factor times a vanishing one:

#% cell
limit(x*sin(1/x), x -> 0)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The two sides of a pole:

#% cell
[limit(1/x, x -> 0, direction: from_above), limit(1/x, x -> 0, direction: from_below)]
#% output
#= [inf, -inf]

#% text
# An essential singularity, with different limits on each side:

#% cell
[limit(exp(1/x), x -> 0, direction: from_above), limit(exp(1/x), x -> 0, direction: from_below)]
#% output
#= [inf, 0]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A derivative from its definition as the limit of a difference quotient:

#% cell
limit(((x + h)^3 - x^3)/h, h -> 0)
#% output
#= 3*x^2

#% text
# The leading behaviour of a difference, through a limit of the ratio:

#% cell
limit((sin(x) - x)/x^3, x -> 0)
#% output
#= -1/6

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The limit agrees with the constant term of the series:

#% cell
[limit((1 - cos(x))/x^2, x -> 0), series((1 - cos(x))/x^2, x, 0, 2)]
#% output
#= [1/2, 1/2 - x^2/24 + O(x^3)]

#% text
# Special functions at an infinite argument:

#% cell
[limit(airy_ai(x), x -> inf), limit(fresnel_s(x), x -> -inf), limit(bessel_j(n, x), x -> inf)]
#% output
#= [0, -1/2, 0]

#% text
# Facts about the parameters with the `assumptions:` option:

#% cell
limit(exp(-k*t), t -> inf, assumptions: k > 0)
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# The direction may also be given as -1 (from above) or 1 (from below):

#% cell
limit(1/x, x -> 0, direction: -1)
#% output
#= inf

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A limit from both sides at a pole of odd order does not exist:

#% cell
limit(1/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, limit, x^-1, "the two-sided limit does not exist: the function is unbounded, tending to inf on one side and -inf on the other")

#% cell
limit(abs(x)/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, limit, abs(x)/x, "the two-sided limit does not exist: the one-sided limits differ")

#% text
# An oscillation has no limit: its limit superior is above its limit inferior; a jump of `floor` has one-sided limits that differ:

#% cell
limit(sin(1/x), x -> 0)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, limit, sin(x^-1), "the limit superior is above the limit inferior")

#% cell
limit(floor(x), x -> 1)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, limit, floor(x), "the two-sided limit does not exist: the one-sided limits differ")

#% text
# `log(x)` at 0 tends to `-inf` from both sides (the imaginary part from below stays bounded); a limit that is unbounded with opposite signs says so in its failure:

#% cell
limit(log(x), x -> 0)
#% output
#= -inf

#% cell
limit(log(x) + tan(x)^3, x -> 0)
#% output
#= -inf

#% cell
limit(1/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, limit, x^-1, "the two-sided limit does not exist: the function is unbounded, tending to inf on one side and -inf on the other")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `series`, `d`, `sum`, `integrate`, `max_limit`, `min_limit`, `discrete_limit`, `asymptotic`
